{"body": "The next inquiry I would pursue is the one J′ itself leaves open: **when P9’s conditional cross-booking incompatibility is “invisible,” is that invisibility a missing observation in the account’s viewpoint, or an overclaim in a projection/aggregation/exactness claim?**\n\nThe relevant original occurrence is the P9 excerpt, especially:\n\n> “booking A’s high alternative might be incompatible with booking B’s high alternative if they share a physical resource not named in the snapshot. The snapshot does not name physical resources, so this incompatibility is invisible.”\n\nThat is paired with J′’s own unresolved remainder:\n\n> “What remains unresolved is whether B’s ‘invisible’ incompatibility is best read as a criticism of the snapshot’s incompleteness or of a projection’s overclaim.”\n\nThe uncertainty is not settled by RSS as given. RSS has a **viewpoint**, a **bag**, a **reading**, a **projection**, an **account**, and **conjectures/commitments**. P9’s conditional says something may be absent or invisible, but it does not say whether the missing element belongs to the bag/viewpoint — an unobserved resource relation — or to the reading/assumption that bookings interact only through capacity, or to the strength of a claim about an interval. Those are different targets in P7’s sense.\n\nThe next inquiry should therefore be:\n\n> For an RSS account α with per-booking alternatives and an interval [L,U], does P9’s compatibility objection question α’s viewpoint/bag for lacking a resource model, α’s reading for assuming independence beyond capacity, or α’s conjecture/commitment for claiming exactness or envelope status for [L,U]?\n\nDifferent outcomes would change the proposed use of J′ in different ways. If the objection is a missing observation/resource model, then J′ should classify it under P7’s target “snapshot as a whole” or under “assumption,” and any resolution would require an external observation or a resource-model addition. That addition is not installed by RSS, and J′ should not pretend it is. If the objection is an overclaim about exactness/envelope, then it targets a conjecture or an aggregation/projection rule, and no missing warehouse fact is required; J′ can keep it within RSS’s conjecture/commitment vocabulary. If both are present, then J′ must not collapse them: the compatibility objection would split into a model-incompleteness branch and a claim-overclaim branch. If neither is warranted, then the compatibility point remains only a conditional caution or open question, not a criticism target at all.\n\nThis inquiry does not certify that C’s criticism of J bears, nor does it reject P9’s compatibility concern. It merely asks what kind of bearing it would have under RSS. The following claims and uses should remain distinguishable throughout:\n\n- P7: criticizing a rule differs from criticizing a record, a booking’s history, the snapshot as a whole, or an assumption.\n- P9: the cross-booking incompatibility is conditional, not an observed warehouse fact.\n- P9: the compatibility/correlation objection is distinct from the downstream-policy objection.\n- RSS: an account is a viewpoint, a reading, and accepted conjectures; projection is separate and single-answer.\n- J′: the bound/envelope/decision distinction belongs to conjecture/commitment vocabulary, not to the projection primitive.\n- E009: the selected prior model criticism remains an unendorsed occurrence, not warehouse evidence and not an RSS authority.\n- No missing compatibility constraint is assumed to exist, and no language extension is installed by asking this question.\n\nThe selected E009 occurrence’s Lean/WHL-specific defects should remain distinct from any RSS claim. If its remarks about non-convexity or cross-booking correlations are used later, they still need a separate showing that they bear on RSS rather than only on the frozen Lean carrier. The question above is warranted because J′ already leaves the RSS-level target unresolved, not because the criticism has been certified.", "commitments": "The next inquiry I would pursue is the one J′ itself leaves open: **when P9’s conditional cross-booking incompatibility is “invisible,” is that invisibility a missing observation in the account’s viewpoint, or an overclaim in a projection/aggregation/exactness claim?**\n\nThe relevant original occurrence is the P9 excerpt, especially:\n\n> “booking A’s high alternative might be incompatible with booking B’s high alternative if they share a physical resource not named in the snapshot. The snapshot does not name physical resources, so this incompatibility is invisible.”\n\nThat is paired with J′’s own unresolved remainder:\n\n> “What remains unresolved is whether B’s ‘invisible’ incompatibility is best read as a criticism of the snapshot’s incompleteness or of a projection’s overclaim.”\n\nThe uncertainty is not settled by RSS as given. RSS has a **viewpoint**, a **bag**, a **reading**, a **projection**, an **account**, and **conjectures/commitments**. P9’s conditional says something may be absent or invisible, but it does not say whether the missing element belongs to the bag/viewpoint — an unobserved resource relation — or to the reading/assumption that bookings interact only through capacity, or to the strength of a claim about an interval. Those are different targets in P7’s sense.\n\nThe next inquiry should therefore be:\n\n> For an RSS account α with per-booking alternatives and an interval [L,U], does P9’s compatibility objection question α’s viewpoint/bag for lacking a resource model, α’s reading for assuming independence beyond capacity, or α’s conjecture/commitment for claiming exactness or envelope status for [L,U]?\n\nDifferent outcomes would change the proposed use of J′ in different ways. If the objection is a missing observation/resource model, then J′ should classify it under P7’s target “snapshot as a whole” or under “assumption,” and any resolution would require an external observation or a r